  "worked_solution": "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our vectors in turn.\n\n- Vector $u$ is *not* a stochastic vector since it contains negative entries.\n\n- Vector $v$ is a stochastic vector. Indeed, \n\n- its entries are all nonnegative, and\n\n- the entries add up to one:\n\n$$\n0.7+0.3=1\n$$\n\n- Vector $w$ is a stochastic vector. Indeed, \n\n- its entries are all nonnegative, and\n\n- the entries add up to one:\n\n$$\n0.65+0.15+0.2=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$v$ and $w$ only.\"",
